6,How does database indexing work?,"Based on the provided arXiv context, database indexing works by managing tuples and indexes across two memory tiers: DDR (Double Data Rate) memory and CXL (CXL Memory) memory. The indexing design determines which indexes are used for lookup, which memory tiers are accessed, and how tuple addresses are resolved, resulting in different data access paths.

The paper proposes five indexing designs for hybrid DDR-CXL memory databases, including single-index and dual-index designs. The single-index design with direct addressing achieves the best performance under most workloads and system configurations, while the dual-index design provides performance advantages mainly when DDR memory distribution is involved.

Database indexing involves two main processes: request execution and data distribution adjustment. Request execution typically involves an index lookup followed by tuple access. Data distribution adjustment affects how indexes and tuples are distributed across the two memory tiers, which in turn affects request execution overhead and data migration overhead.

The paper also discusses the importance of considering both indexing design and data distribution in database performance optimization. The optimal indexing design can vary depending on the workload, memory capacity, and memory access performance.

