13,How can researchers evaluate particle acceleration?,"Based on the provided context from arXiv, researchers can evaluate particle acceleration through numerical simulations and analysis of the resulting particle spectra. The studies mentioned in the context, such as those by Prete et al. (2026b) and Li et al. (2025), employed numerical codes to simulate the dynamics of protons around a planar shock wave and studied the acceleration process.

The simulations used test particles moving in the drift approximation along the magnetic field, which is taken to be perpendicular to the planar shock surface. The acceleration mechanism was found to depend on the shock geometry, turbulence level, and particle properties.

By analyzing the particle spectra, researchers can estimate the efficiency of particle acceleration, the maximum energy reached by particles, and the percentage of particles that overcame the detection threshold. The results can provide insights into the physics of particle acceleration at interplanetary shocks.

In particular, the study by Prete et al. (2026b) evaluated the percentage of particles that overcame the energy threshold and found that an increase in the exponent of superdiffusion leads to a lower particle acceleration efficiency. They also found that the maximum energy reached by particles depends on the shock speed and the gas compression ratio.

Furthermore, the simulations can be used to compare different acceleration mechanisms, such as first-order Fermi acceleration and shock drift acceleration, and to study the influence of scattering times and shock parameters on the acceleration process.

In summary, researchers can evaluate particle acceleration through numerical simulations and analysis of the resulting particle spectra, and by using these results to estimate the efficiency of particle acceleration, the maximum energy reached by particles, and the percentage of particles that overcame the detection threshold.

