27,How does vector databases work?,"Vector databases work by partitioning data across ""shards"" to increase capacity and scalability. Each shard stores a subset of the data in one or more segments, with each segment maintaining its own index. The data is partitioned using a hashing technique, which assigns vectors to shards based on their identifiers. This approach is designed to be computationally inexpensive and provides balanced data placement, but it loses semantic locality, meaning that evidence relevant to one question may be scattered across different shards.

To address this limitation, some vector databases use a knowledge graph (KG) to determine data placement and query-time shard selection. A KG is a structured representation of domain-specific knowledge, which can provide a complementary placement signal to the hashing technique. The KG is used to detect communities, split oversized communities, and insert embeddings by subject entity. The system then routes queries to a small set of shards, which improves performance and reduces the need for scatter-gather operations.

One vector database, COMPASS, uses a KG to guide data placement and shard selection. COMPASS detects graph communities, splits oversized communities, and inserts embeddings by subject entity. It then routes queries to a small set of shards, which improves performance and reduces the need for scatter-gather operations. COMPASS has been evaluated on four biomedical KGs and has shown improved performance and efficiency compared to traditional embedding-based methods.

Overall, vector databases work by partitioning data across shards using a hashing technique, but the use of a KG can provide a more effective and efficient way to manage data and improve query performance.

