93,How does operating system scheduling work?,"Based on the provided context from arXiv, operating system scheduling involves the allocation of processing time to tasks or threads. The scheduling algorithm determines the order in which tasks are executed.

In the context of Awkernel, the operating system scheduling is implemented as a scheduler object that owns a run queue and implements a scheduling rule. The scheduler object is a singleton that is instantiated per scheduling algorithm, and it has a fixed precedence order. The scheduler invocations occur at two opportunities: (i) on wake (by a publisher, sleep-timer expiration, or IPI), the wake site calls wake_task() on the appropriate scheduler object, which enqueues the woken subtask onto the run queue of that scheduler; (ii) at the top of the run loop of each per-core worker, it calls get_next_task() to dequeue the highest-priority runnable subtask, consulting scheduler objects in the fixed precedence order.

The scheduling algorithm used by Awkernel is based on the SCHED_DEADLINE policy of Linux, which implements the constant bandwidth server (CBS) algorithm combined with EDF. This policy is designed to provide a natural choice for approximating EDF-based scheduling in ROS 2.

It's worth noting that the operating system scheduling in Awkernel is designed to minimize the overheads caused by divergence between the model and runtime behavior, and to support arbitrary DAG scheduling algorithms and their parameters. The platform must provide sufficient freedom to describe arbitrary DAG scheduling algorithms and their parameters, and must support every mechanism arbitrary DAG scheduling algorithms require.

