99,How can researchers evaluate vector databases?,"Based on the provided context from arXiv, researchers can evaluate vector databases by using benchmark studies that analyze the performance of different index structures, distance comparison operations (DCOs), and algorithms. These studies can identify the merits and limits of various approaches, including their efficiency, scalability, and robustness to out-of-distribution queries.

Some key evaluation protocols used in these studies include:

1. Evaluating the efficiency of different DCO methods, such as Full Dimension Scanning (FDScanning) and Partial Dimension Scanning (PDScanning), and comparing their performance to state-of-the-art algorithms.
2. Analyzing the accuracy and recall of different index structures, such as graph-based indexes, partition-based indexes, and hash-based indexes.
3. Assessing the scalability of vector databases and their ability to handle large-scale datasets.
4. Evaluating the robustness of vector databases to out-of-distribution queries and their ability to handle distribution shifts in query vectors.

To conduct a comprehensive evaluation, researchers can use benchmarking tools, such as the DCO-benchmark, which provides a set of pre-defined queries and datasets to compare the performance of different algorithms.

